Hi David,

This blog post <http://gforge.se/2013/02/exporting-nice-plots-in-r/> might be of some use regarding saving plots in R. If you are working with illustrator, I would highly recommend saving your plots in SVG format. This allows you to manipulate them quite easily as it is a format that illustrator can accept natively <http://help.adobe.com/en_US/illustrator/cs/using/WS714a382cdf7d304e7e07d0100196cbc5f-6360a.html#WS714a382cdf7d304e7e07d0100196cbc5f-635fa>. It is true that *.ps files work relatively well and *.emf files are specifically for working in MS Word, but nothing gives you as much control as an SVG file.

In R, you can save plots directly from the command line like so:

svg(filename = "myPlot.svg", width = 5, height = 3, pointsize = 12)

pcl2013.msn = bruvo.msn(pcl2013, replen=c(2,2,3,3,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2), vertex.label = "inds", vertex.label.family = "sans", wscale = FALSE)

dev.off()

Note that you can replace svg with png, pdf, jpg, ... etc. The advantage of exporting to a vector graphics format like svg or pdf is that you can edit different layers of the plot. In illustrator, you can do this by ungrouping or expanding objects <http://help.adobe.com/en_US/illustrator/cs/using/WS714a382cdf7d304e7e07d0100196cbc5f-62c7a.html#WS714a382cdf7d304e7e07d0100196cbc5f-62c4a>.

I would also like to point out the function called "plot_poppr_msn", which allows you to manipulate the layout of the graph and also plots a scale bar. The inputs are your original data set and then the graph produced from bruvo.msn.

Please let me know if any of this has been helpful.

Best,
Zhian

